l result then  immediately implies that no 2qsfa can  recognize palindrome in sub-exponential  time  now in order to put our lower ground for  2qcfa in perspective  it's natural to compare to the analysis  result for 2pfa proven by dork and  stockmeyer  who show that the running time of any  2pfa is lower bounded by an exponential  in their art of measure where we note  that  both of these lower bounds are  essentially tight that is the dwark  stockmar lower bound for 2pfa is  exponentially stronger than our lower  bound for 2q cfa  however this exponential difference  can't be helped  this is merely yet another example of a  setting in which quantum computers  exponentially outperform classical  appears  provably now all of the results stated  so far  apply to quantum finite automata which  are just quantum computers that have  only a constant amount of space  we next observe that the same technique  also yields results for quantum  computers that use a  small amount of space in particular  consider a quantum turing machine leaves  a space s n which is sub-logarithmic  that is little o log n space or if you  prefer  you can consider the more standard but  equivalent quantum circuit model  in which case this is just a quantum  circuit on smn cubits  then for any language we prove a lower  bound on the running time of any such  machine that recognizes that language  where now this lower bound is expressed  in terms of both the non-regularity of  the language  and the amount of space we allow the  machine to use  in the interest of brevity we'll just  consider one example the palindro  language  again here we prove that no  sub-logarithmic space quantum computer  can recognize palindrome even if given  time two to the n to the one minus  epsilon  to put that lower bound in perspective  note that even a deterministic computer  can easily recognize palindrome in log  space and polytime  and so a quantum computer can certainly  do the same but our lower bound says the  quantum computer can't do any better  if one wants to recognize palindrome and  polytime then log space is necessary  to provide further context for our lower  bound recall that  any language that can be recognized by a  quantum computer in log space can also  be recognized by a deterministic  computer in log squared space  both this results in our result provide  strong limitations on quantum advantage  in the small space setting  but the exact details of those  limitations are incomparable  next i want to discuss the key tool that  was used to prove  all the results mentioned so far which  is a quantum analog  of the classical crossing sequence  argument  now crossing sequences are an ancient  technique  defined originally by henny in 1965  who used them to prove lower bounds  against deterministic turing machines  for example what can show that any  language that can be recognized in time  little o and log n  must be right penny's original  deterministic crossing sequence  technique was then generalized to  probabilistic computation  by dwergan stockmeyer and greenberg and  weiss which enabled them to prove their  lower bounds against two pfa that were  stated earlier  i can now generalize this technique even  further to quantum computation  and then using these quantum crossing  sequences i can prove the various lower  bounds against 2qcfa  and small space quantum computers that  were stated earlier  the remainder of this talk will be  devoted to explaining this notion of  crossing sequences  first i'll briefly sketch henny's  original deterministic crossing sequence  argument  and then i'll provide a high level  overview of my quantum analog  suppose we have a deterministic turing  machine m  and suppose we also have an input string  that's been partitioned into two pieces  the left part of the string will call x  and the right part we call y  we then run m on the input x y and we  consider the path  m's head takes during this computation  so in this example the head first  meanders around the x part of the string  for a while changing direction multiple  times  before the first time it crosses into  the y part of the string and it moves  around the y part of the string for a  while  crossing back to the x part of the  string it moves around the x part of the  string for a while crossing back into  the y part of the string  and so on we then  keep track of the state the finite  control of m is in  each time m's head crosses the boundary  between x and y  so the first time we cross from x into y  the finding control is in state c1  then we cross from y back into x and  state c2 and so on  we then define the crossing sequence of  m on the partitioned input x y  as the sequence of states c1 c2 and so  on that m is in  and its various crossings in the  boundary  so that's the definition of a crossing  sequence  next we will see how crossing sequences  can be used to prove lower bounds  let's now consider running m on a pair  of partition inputs  x y and x y prime where it's only the  suffixes of these two strings that  differ  suppose that the crossing sequence of m  when it's run on the input x  y is identical to the crossing sequence  of m on x y prime  then we claim that m has to make the  same decision on x y and x y prime  that is both of these inputs can be  accepted or both have to be rejected  let's see why that's the case we  consider running m  on x y and on x y prime  the goal will be to understand the  relationship between these two  computations  let's start by thinking about the  initial segment of the computation of m  on the input x y where here we pause the  computation  as soon as m's head crosses the boundary  into the suffix y if we then consider  the analogous initial segment of the  computation of  m on x y prime we immediately see that  these two initial segments  must be identical simply because these  two inputs  share the same prefix x  we then consider the second segment the  computation  of m on the input x y  where now we pause this computation  after the next boundary crossing  if we then consider the analogous second  segment of the computation of  m on the input x y prime this part of  the computation might look completely  different  as the suffix y and the suffix y prime  might be very different strings  however by our assumption that the  crossing sequences are identical  we can conclude that m is in the same  state c2  after the second crossing in both cases  continuing this process we then consider  the third segment  the computation in each of these two  cases and we observe that these segments  must be identical this follows from the  fact that at the beginning of the third  segment  the relevant portion of the  configuration of m is exactly the same  in both cases this is say  the finding control of m is in the same  state c2  head is in the same position one cell to  the left of the boundary  and the portion of the tape to the left  of the boundary has the same contents in  both cases  of course this may no longer be the  string x as m could have overwritten  part of that string during the first  segment of this computation  but as those first segments were  identical the string  in the left part of the tape will be  identical in the two cases and so the  third segment the computation will be  identical in the two cases  continuing we then consider the fourth  segment of the computation  of m on x y and the fourth segment of  the computation of m  on x y prime which again might look  completely different  but by our assumption that the crossing  sequences are identical  we know that m is in the same state c4  after the fourth crossing  in both cases we then continue this  process  until m eventually holds on the input x  y  in this example that happens during the  fifth segment  and m accepts by the same arguments  before  the corresponding segment of m's  computation on x y prime must be  identical  and so m must accept x y prime as  desired  the same argument proves the claim in  the general case  identical crossing sequences imply  identical answers  next we consider a lower bound technique  that makes use of this claim  suppose we have a hard language l  and suppose we also have a deterministic  turing machine m  that recognizes l quickly where i won't  be at all specific about the  hardness measure or running time bound  be considered here  what we would like to do is find a bad  pair of partition strings x y and x y  prime  such that m has to make mistake on one  of them  thereby contradicting the assumption  that m recognizes l quickly  to accomplish this we require one of the  strings to be in the language  and the other string to be not in the  language and we also require  m to have identical crossing sequences  on these two strings  if these conditions are satisfied then  by the previous claim  m must make the same decision on these  two strings  but only one of them is in language and  so m  must be wrong on one of these two  strings  next i will give an overview of my  quantum version of crossing sequences  here we consider running a 2qcfa m  on a partitioned input x y  now of course the basic setup here is  quite similar to that of the  deterministic case that we've just  considered  however the fact that we are now working  with a quantum model of computation  adds all sorts of new difficulties  in particular suppose we try to mimic  the definition of deterministic crossing  sequences  by considering the path the head takes  during this computation  then we immediately run to a problem as  quantum measurement is a probabilistic  process  and so whenever a 2q cfa performs a  quantum measurement  the machine probabilistically branches  according to the different possible  measurement outcomes  and so what we've really illustrated  here is the path taking by the head  in a single branch of the computation  moreover even if we ignore this issue at  the moment  and just fix our attention on one single  branch of the computation  we then immediately run to a new problem  suppose we try to keep track of m state  every time this single branch crosses  the boundary between x and y  in the previous deterministic case this  only entailed keeping track of the state  of the finite control of the machine  however now in the current 2q cfa case  we have to deal with a much more  complicated notion of state  as we have to consider both the classic  state of the finding control  and also the current contents of the  quantum register  which is described by a quantum state  and so we immediately have these two  serious issues  the probabilistic computation tree and  this more complicated notion of state  which we will have to deal with in order  to define a quantum analog of crossing  sequences  though other issues will also arise  later in the analysis  we begin by considering the structure of  a 2q cfa's probabilistic computation  tree  as before we run m on the input xy  the total configuration of m at any  particular point in time  and on any particular branch of the  computation is described by a triple  consisting of the current classic state  the current quantum state  and the current head position each node  of the computation tree  consists of a single configuration in  particular the root of the tree  is simply m's configuration at the very  beginning of the computation  in order to populate the rest of the  tree we run m one step at a time  each step of the computation involves  either applying a unitary transformation  to the quantum register  or performing a quantum measurement as  well as also  updating the classic state and moving  the head one cell in either direction  the choice of which operation to perform  at any given step  depends only on the current classic  state and the input symbol that's  currently under the head  suppose for example that the first step  of the computation  involves applying a unitary  transformation  then the initial configuration will have  a single child  given by the configuration that m will  be in after that transformation  suppose the next step of the computation  involves instead  performing quantum measurement then for  each possible outcome of that  measurement  the current configuration will have a  child given by the configuration that m  would be in  assuming that measurement outcome occurs  now of course different measurement  outcomes may occur with different  probabilities  and so each child may be reached with a  different probability  continuing this process one step further  it may be the case that in one  configuration  a quantum measurement is performed  whereas in a different configuration a  unitary transformation is formed  next we'll use these computation trees  to define a  stopping ensemble of a 2q cfa which is a  quantum analog of  torque and stockmeyer's notion of a  stopping ensemble of a 2pfa  as before we consider running m on the  input xy  but now we only run m until it has  finished computing on the prefix x  where we say that a particular branch of  the computation has finished computing  on x  as soon as that branch crosses into the  y part of the input  or accepts or rejects  to illustrate this idea let's consider  an example  of a possible computation of m one step  at a time  we start with the initial configuration  of m the configuration after one step  the collection of possible  configurations after two steps and then  the collection of possible  configurations after three steps where  in this example  the leftmost branch the computation has  accepted and so  has finished computing we then continue  running the remaining branches  and after the next step the rightmost  branch of the computation  has crossed from the x part of the input  into the y part of the input  and so that branch has finished  computing on x  we then pause that branch the  computation  in our analysis we don't modify the  behavior of the machine  and then we continue running the  remaining branches  after a while all the branches in this  example computation  have finished computed on x  the stopping ensemble consists of  the collection of all terminal  configurations from this process  where we also keep track of the  probability with which each of these  configurations is reached  now i am glossing over certain details  here for example  a given branch might run forever without  crossing  but those issues can be dealt with  we will now describe the entire  computation of m on the input x y  by repeatedly applying the idea from the  previous slide  we begin by considering the initial  configuration of m  and we define the first starting  ensemble to consist of just this one  single configuration  we then run m until it has finished  computing on the prefix x  at which point we have our first  stopping ensemble  a stopping ensemble contains accepting  projecting configurations  which correspond to branches of the  computation which are completely  finished  but it also contains crossing  configurations  which correspond to branches that were  paused as soon as they entered y  we now want to allow those branches to  resume  in order to do this we define a second  starting ensemble  which is equal the first stopping  ensemble  we then run m until it's finished  computing on the suffix y  where now everything is defined  symmetrically that is we run each branch  till it accepts rejects or re-enters x  we then obtain our second stopping  ensemble  and then we keep going now  in order to motivate what's coming next  notice that the process that transforms  an arbitrary starting ensemble to its  corresponding stopping  ensemble has a very nice description  namely  all we do is run each branch until it  accepts  rejects or crosses the boundary between  x and y  once therefore  the first stopping ensemble corresponds  to  allowing each branch to cross the  boundary once  the second stopping ensemble corresponds  to allowing each branch cross twice  and so on this naturally suggests using  the sequence of samples to define  a crossing sequence unfortunately  there's a problem  these ensembles are very messy objects  which  simply aren't suited for the kind of  analysis one would like to perform in a  crossing sequence based lower bound  argument  to avoid this problem we replace each of  these ensembles  with the density operator that describes  it where of course many different  ensembles are described the same density  operator  but they are all equivalent for our  purposes  we then define the crossing sequence as  the sequence of density operators  corresponding to the ensembles we saw in  the previous slide  to be precise the first density operator  describes the first starting ensemble  the second density operator describes  the first stopping ensemble which is the  second starting ensemble  and so on next i want to make one  crucial definition related to crossing  sequences which is that of transfer  operator  this corresponds to running m until it  is finished computing on the prefix  x to be a bit more precise  these operators are maps from density  operators to density operators  which convert any starting ensemble into  its corresponding and stop the ensemble  for example the second element of the  crossing sequence is the result of  applying this operator to the first  element  of course one always can divide an  analogous notion for sum x y  now there are just two important  observations i want to make about  transfer operators  the first is that they describe the  behavior of the machine  on just a piece of the input a prefix or  suffix  and the second is that all these  operators are quantum channels  which plays an important role in the  analysis  i conclude by sketching my lower bound  argument  we need a few definitions firstly for  language l  we say that a pair of strings x x prime  is  ln dissimilar if there's a suffix y  such the strings x y and x prime y both  have lend the most n  and exactly one of these strings is in  language  the dwark stock buyer non-regularity of  l is given by this function d sub l  where d sub l of n is the maximum number  of pairwise ln to similar strings  now suppose we have a 2q cfa  m that recognizes l in time t of n  we establish a lower bound on the  running time as follows  we prove that there's a pair of input  prefixes  x and x prime which are ln dissimilar  and the distance between their transfer  operators  is upper bounded by this particular  expression involving the non-regularity  of l  let's consider the consequences of those  two conditions  we run m on the input x y  and x prime y for some suffix y which is  a witness  of l and the similarity by definition  exactly one of these inputs should be  accepted by m  and m of running time t of n on both of  them  we then consider the transfer operators  where as we just discussed  these correspond to running m until it  is finished computing on one of the  prefixes  x or x prime if the distance between  these transfer operators is small  then on any single visit to one of x or  x  prime m will have to behave similarly  but then if the running time is small m  can't possibly make too many visits to  its prefix string  and so now m will have to behave  similarly on x y  and x prime y in particular m  will have to accept these inputs with  similar probability which is impossible  because exactly one of these strings is  in language  therefore the running time must be large  thank you for listening
 

